The '''Nelvaanians''', or '''Nelvaans''', were a race of blue-furred [[Wolf/Legends|lupine]] [[Humanoid/Legends|humanoids]] native to the [[Planet/Legends|planet]] [[Nelvaan/Legends|Nelvaan]]. The Nelvaanians called their planet the 'Great Mother', which they worshiped.

Nelvaanian [[Gender/Legends|males]] became warriors and scouts while the females were builders, gatherers, and raised the children. The tribe was governed by one [[Shaman/Legends|shaman]] who claimed spiritual rites within a clan, and one [[Chief/Legends|chieftain]], elected through a series of religious tests and mutual admiration.

==History==
[[File:Nelvaanian_hunter_TCWCG.jpg|thumb|left|A Nelvaanian hunter stands over a destroyed [[B1-series battle droid/Legends|B1-Series battle droid]]]]
During the [[Clone Wars/Legends|Clone Wars]], Nelvaan (a planet situated near the [[Corellian Run/Legends|Corellian Run]] in the galaxy's [[Savareen sector/Legends|Savareen sector]]) was the location of a [[Confederacy of Independent Systems/Legends|Separatist]] base run by members of the [[Techno Union/Legends|Techno Union]]. [[General/Legends|General]] [[Grievous/Legends|Grievous]] lured warriors from the [[Rokrul]] village by having them believe he was a prophesied figure out of Nelvaan mythology, as had been spoken of by their village [[Shaman/Legends|shaman]] [[Orvos]]. Techno Union scientists then captured them, and physically and mentally altered them into [[Cyborg/Legends|cyborgs]] for the war.

Grievous, who served as [[Supreme Commander of the Droid Armies/Legends|Supreme Commander of the Separatist droid army]], had been appraised of the [[Outer Rim Territories/Legends|Outer Rim]] planet by Techno Union foreman [[Wat Tambor]]. Upon visiting Nelvaan, Grievous was very much impressed by the similarities between the spiritual Nelvaanian warriors and his own [[Kaleesh/Legends|Kaleesh]] people and, in a twisted form of kinship, he intended to make them his 'new elite', harking back to his earlier days as commander of the elite '[[Izvoshra/Legends|Izvoshra']] soldiers of his people's [[Kolkpravis]] warrior band. It was General Grievous who thus lured the male Nelvaanian soldiers away from their tribe by making them believe he was the Nelvaan mythology's prophesied figure in order to conduct -- via [[Combat engineer (Techno Union)/Legends|Skakoan combat engineers]] using 'unique microelectronics' -- a crude [[cybernetics]] experiment upon their bodies.<ref name="Blog 55">{{Blog|abelgpena|55|Endnotes for The Story of General Grievous, Part 2: The Knight Slayer}}</ref> By using samples of [[3L41UH7 serum]] provided to him by the mad [[Geneticist/Legends|geneticist]] [[Zeta Magnus]], General Grievous was able to create a small army of hulking mutants.<ref name="Skyewalkers">''[[SkyeWalkers: A Clone Wars Story]]''</ref>  But the perverse mutation project was ultimately sabotaged by [[Anakin Skywalker/Legends|Anakin Skywalker]], who [[Jedi Trials/Legends|heroically freed]] the warriors from what otherwise would have been a hideous fate.<ref name="Blog 55" />

After freeing the soldiers in what came to be known as the [[Battle of Nelvaan]], Skywalker, who was referred to by the Nelvaan race as [[Holt Kazed|''Holt Kezed'']] (''Ghost Hand'' in their language, because of his mechanical right arm), destroyed the Separatist base. Although initially frightened by the near-miraculous reappearance of their warriors, the Nelvaanian females and children came quickly to accept them, joyously welcoming them back into the tribe.

==Behind the scenes==
The Nelvaan people first appeared in ''[[Star Wars: Clone Wars]]''. In the commentary, [[Genndy Tartakovsky]] revealed that the Nelvaan language is partially [[Wikipedia:Hungarian language|Hungarian]]. The appearance of Nelvaanians was inspired by the "dog-headed people" from the 1983 [[Nelvana]] film [[Wikipedia:Rock & Rule|''Rock & Rule'']].<ref name="CWVol2">[[Star Wars: Clone Wars Volume Two|''Star Wars: Clone Wars'' Volume Two]] audio commentary</ref>

